WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Picture hydrogen the simple way:
one electron circling one proton.

2
00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:08.130
Electric attraction
holds it on its circle.

3
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.420
Classical physics allows any
radius, and so any energy.

4
00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.760
It also predicts disaster.

5
00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:18.110
An orbiting charge
radiates, and spirals in.

6
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.060
Real atoms do not.

7
00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.960
In 1913, Bohr allowed only certain orbits.

8
00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:27.660
But why can't the electron
orbit at any distance it likes?

9
00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.140
In this mission, you'll
follow Bohr's rule,

10
00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.940
and turn his allowed orbits
into hydrogen's energy levels.

11
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.730
Then you'll judge the
model: what it got right,

12
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.200
like the 13.6 electronvolt energy
needed to free the electron,

13
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.070
and where it fails.

14
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.770
Your mission: explain why the electron in
hydrogen can occupy only certain orbits.
